# Vergence release signers, in ssh-keygen allowed_signers format.
#
# Every Vergence release is one SHA256SUMS file listing its assets, signed with
# `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n vergence-release` (SSHSIG; `desktop/scripts/bless-release.sh` does it
# and checks the result). The desktop app's updater verifies a download against these keys,
# compiled in, before using it, and refuses anything the signature doesn't cover. Download URLs
# are not trusted at all: an asset can come from any host as long as its hash is in a signed
# manifest.
#
# TWO KEYS, both pinned from the first release that ships this file: the everyday hardware key
# (needs a PIN and a touch, never on disk or in CI) and an offline backup, for when the
# hardware key is lost or broken. A backup added later would be worthless: nothing already
# installed would trust it, and the release introducing it couldn't be verified.
#
# They are the same two keys that sign Apparition's and Longwave's releases (Apparition's
# `release-signers`, MIT, same author), so there is one pair to protect. The namespace differs
# ("vergence-release"), so a signature made for either of those can't pass as a Vergence
# release, nor one of Vergence's as theirs.
releases@vergence ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIPDFLEggFzedj9nvz0wl9AoPGXFFbT9XfgEWrAY0l7pa ixion@YubiKey5-gpg
